      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Middletown’s roots trace back to the Pomo and Lake Miwok peoples, whose descendants now live on the 108‑acre Middletown Rancheria.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/Middletown,_California",
      "category": "people",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In September 2015 the Valley Fire swept through Middletown, forcing the evacuation of its roughly 1,300 residents.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/Middletown,_California",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Before becoming Middletown, the community was known as Middle Station, a midway stop on the stagecoach route over the mountains.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        29
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Middletown%2C_California",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "European‑American settlement in the Middletown area began in the mid‑19th century, marking the town’s formal founding.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
